    "words": 19,
    "wpm": 204,
    "pause_after": 0.6,
    "think": 0.0,
    "text": "A banyan tree can weigh more than a herd of elephants. So where did all that wood come from?"
   },
   {
    "id": "b02",
    "slot": "check.predict",
    "start": 6.8,
    "end": 13.76,
    "audio_s": 6.96,
    "words": 19,
    "wpm": 164,
    "pause_after": 0.0,
    "think": 5.0,
    "text": "Where did the carbon in that wood come from: the soil, the water, or the air? Pause and predict."
   },
   {
    "id": "b03",
    "slot": "explain.ladder_step",
    "start": 18.76,
    "end": 27.48,
    "audio_s": 8.72,
    "words": 23,
    "wpm": 158,
    "pause_after": 0.5,
    "think": 0.0,
    "text": "Green plants make their own food. Carbon dioxide enters from the air through stomata, and water comes up from the soil through roots."
   },
   {
    "id": "b04",
    "slot": "explain.ladder_step",
    "start": 27.98,
    "end": 39.02,
    "audio_s": 11.04,
    "words": 23,
    "wpm": 125,
    "pause_after": 0.8,
    "think": 0.0,
    "text": "Sunlight, absorbed by chlorophyll, drives the change, making glucose and oxygen. Count the carbon: six atoms on each side, all from carbon dioxide."
   },
   {
    "id": "b05",
    "slot": "misconception.confront",
    "start": 39.82,
    "end": 51.74,
    "audio_s": 11.92,
    "words": 32,
    "wpm": 161,
    "pause_after": 0.6,
    "think": 0.0,
    "text": "Many think a plant is built from soil. But soil mainly gives water and minerals; the carbon in wood comes from the air. About forty percent of glucose, by mass, is carbon."
   },
   {
    "id": "b06",
    "slot": "recap.statement",
    "start": 52.34,
    "end": 60.58,
    "audio_s": 8.24,
    "words": 20,
    "wpm": 146,
    "pause_after": 0.6,
    "think": 0.0,
    "text": "So a tree's carbon came from the air. Carbon dioxide and water, with sunlight and chlorophyll, make glucose and oxygen."
   },
